# DIAGNOSTICS

Without its registers, interrupt or listen channel it prints `drv-uart-pl011: no registers, IRQ or listen channel` and exits with the words after the colon.
If it cannot wait for its interrupt it prints `drv-uart-pl011: cannot wait for the IRQ` and exits so; if serving fails it exits `cannot serve`.

# BUGS

The speed and line format are never set, so the console works only at what the firmware or the bootloader left.
There is no raw mode and no `consctl`: every program reads cooked lines.
`^C` is dropped rather than sent as a note, so it cannot interrupt a program.
A blocked read of the console is not interrupted by a signal: its handler runs once the line comes.
There is no `/dev/cons` in a namespace: the console is a handle given at spawn, so a program cannot open it by name.
Overrun, parity, framing and break errors are ignored; there is no flow control.
The manifest names the UART's address, so it is QEMU's `virt` machine's; the PL011 that ACPI's tables list is not matched from them.
